===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To use the foot to [[kick]] (usually a [[ball]]).
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[pay]] (a bill).
# To tread to measure of music; to dance; to trip; to skip.
#* '''1836''', {{w|Joanna Baillie}}, ''The Phantom'', Act 1 (''Dramas 2'', p.217)
#*: There's time enough, I hope, To '''foot''' a measure with the bonnie bride,
#* {{RQ:Dryden Fables||The Wife of Bath's Tale|passage=He saw a Quire of Ladies in a round, <br>That featly '''footing''' seem'd to skim the Ground}}
# To walk.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merry Wives|2|1|passage=thieves do '''foot''' by night}}
# {{lb|en|now|rare}} To [[set foot]] on; to walk on.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1722|author=w:Thomas Tickell|title=Kensington Gardens|passage={{...}} Or shepherd-boy, they featly '''foot''' the green}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1937|author=w:Zora Neale Hurston|title=Their Eyes Were Watching God|publisher=Amistad|year_published=2013|page=84
|passage=People who would not have dared to '''foot''' the place before crept in and did not come to the house.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To set on foot; to establish; to land.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King Lear|III|vii|passage=What confederacy have you with the traitors / Late '''footed''' in the kingdom?}}
# To renew the foot of (a stocking, etc.).
#* '''19th century''', {{w|William Shakespeare}}, ''Henry the Fourth Part 1'' (modern edited version)
#*: {{quote|en|I'll sew nether stocks and mend them and '''foot''' them too}}
# To sum up, as the numbers in a column; sometimes with ''up''.
#: {{ux|en|to '''foot''' (or foot up) an account}}
# {{lb|en|Ireland|transitive}} To [[spread out]] and [[stack up]] ([[turf]] [[sod]]s) to allow them to [[dry]].

